 {:name "correct-a93a01-direct-uniform-continuity-quotient-argument"
  :level :challenge
  :status :draft
  :body
  {:challenge-kind :corpus-correction
   :target
   {:artifact "problems/a93A01/informal-solution.md"
    :direction "(a) implies (b)"
    :challenged-claim
    "After uniform continuity supplies δ for ε, taking `N = ε / δ` directly proves that a quotient larger than N forces `|f(x)-f(y)| < ε`."}
   :verdict :mathematically-invalid
   :error
   "The quotient inequality alone does not bound its numerator. A large value of `|f(x)-f(y)| / |x-y|` can coexist with `|x-y| ≥ δ` when `|f(x)-f(y)|` is large, so the proposed direct contradiction does not follow. The artifact's later sequence variant has the same gap: `|xₙ-yₙ| < |f(xₙ)-f(yₙ)| / n` does not imply `|xₙ-yₙ| → 0` unless the numerators are uniformly bounded."
   :correction
   {:architecture :boundedness-first
    :steps
    ["Use uniform continuity on the totally bounded interval `(0,1)` to prove the image is bounded."
     "Choose a positive B' with `|f x| ≤ B'`, hence `|f x - f y| ≤ 2B'`."
     "For the δ supplied by uniform continuity at ε, take `N = 2B' / δ`."
     "A quotient larger than N now forces `|x-y| < δ`, and uniform continuity supplies the desired numerator bound."]}
   :scope
   "This challenge is limited to the harvested proof of `(a) implies (b)`. The reverse implication's use of `δ = ε / N`, after condition (b) supplies N, is valid."
   :witness
   {:formalization-commit "035e4d97175ecc2b9dbbeebf72d734326c67b9e3"
    :validation "Lean exit 0 with zero errors and zero sorries"
    :corrected-theorem "unifCont_implies_diffQuot"}}
